And yet, for a situation such as a cockatrice corpse, there is a small,
finite set of interactions which must be solved to use one safely.  You
could even write it on a Post-It(tm) note and stick it to your monitor.

Creating a situation where solving the small, finite set of interactions
allows one to remove the vast majority of those other interactions is
shoddy game design.  Making rotproof footrice corpses is one such
situation.  (And indeed, there are already such situations in Nethack --
the single intrinsic "reflection" is an excellent example.)

>> The mass superstition that the RNG has anything at all to do with an
>> individual player's death can be amusing at times, but when considering
>> questions of game balance it should be discarded as exactly that:
>> superstition.
> 
>   Many times I've recognized a stupid mistake just after I've made it
> and only the mercy of the RNG kept it from being fatal.  When every
> player is perfect then RNG can be considered a superstition, not until
