Blather.  Every player has the _potential_ to be perfect; it is a
turn-based game for which many written references are available,
including the code itself.  As such, any failure to be perfect is
nothing more than an instance of a mistake by the player.

The game is designed well, and as such the vast majority of mistakes
will not instantly kill you; however, some will, and it is part of the
evolution of skill for each player to slowly learn to recognize the key
mistakes and remove them from his/her play.

As a player gains in skill, they make fewer mistakes, and thus die less.
 The RNG has almost nothing to do with it; the player's skill, on the
other hand, has everything to do with it.  Pretending that the RNG is
somehow involved in anything other than the extreme corner cases
(polytrap on DL8 out of your sight, hobbit jumps on it, turns into
arch-lich, teleports next turn and touch-of-deaths you, DYWYPI) is still
just self-limiting superstition.

